============================================================================================
                                    SetProject
============================================================================================
This project implements a generic Set Abstract Data Type (ADT) using a dynamic array.
The set stores unique elements and maintains them in ascending order using the 'Comparable' interface.
The program reads student records from a text file, stores them in the set, prevents duplicate student
IDs, and demonstrates basic set operations such as adding, searching, removing, and iterating through elements.



Class
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. SetADT: An interface that defines the basic operations supported by the Set ADT, including
            adding, removing, searching, checking the size, checking whether the set is empty,
            and clearing the set. It also extends `Iterable` to allow elements to be accessed
            using an enhanced for-loop.

2. AbstractSet: A base class that provides common functionality for set implementations.
            It maintains the number of elements in the set and implements shared methods such
            as 'size()' and 'isEmpty()'.

3. ArraySet: The main implementation of the Set ADT. It uses a dynamic array to store elements
            in ascending order. It prevents duplicate elements, automatically expands its capacity
            when necessary, and supports adding, removing, searching, and clearing elements.

4. SetIterator: A custom iterator that allows elements to be accessed one at a time, starting
            from the first element and continuing to the last. It implements 'hasNext()' and 'next()'
            to support sequential traversal of the set.

5. Student: Represents a student with an ID, name, and GPA. It implements 'Comparable<Student>' to compare
            students by ID and determine their position in the set. It also overrides 'equals()' and 'hashCode()'
            so that students with the same ID are considered equal, regardless of their names or GPAs.

6. Main: The entry point of the program. It reads student records from a text file, creates Student objects,
            and adds them to the set. It also demonstrates displaying elements, searching for a student,
            removing a student, and checking the total number of elements and array capacity.

Input File
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Student.txt : The input file contains student records in CSV format:
              [Student ID, Student Name, GPA].
              The program reads each record, extracts its values, and creates a corresponding Student object.
              Duplicate student IDs are rejected by the set.

Features
------------------------------------------------------------
    - Stores unique elements.
    - Maintains elements in ascending order.
    - Uses 'Comparable' to determine element order.
    - Automatically expands the array capacity when necessary.
    - Supports adding, removing, searching, and clearing elements.
    - Implements a custom iterator.
    - Reads and processes student records from a text file.
    - Prevents duplicate students based on student ID.

Expected Behavior
------------------------------------------------------------
When the program runs, it displays the student records in ascending order by ID. Duplicate student IDs are rejected,
and the program demonstrates searching for student ID 1005 and removing student ID 1003.
The program also displays the total number of students and the current array capacity.